(6866242, 1033356, 42013, '<td valign="top">My personal high score from the challenge. Glad you ribboned!</td>', 'My personal high score from the challenge. Glad you ribboned!', '2012-09-19 16:21:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(6866252, 1033013, 77196, '<td valign="top">IMO, your scoring on this got hammered for three main reasons:<br/>1) Not what the voters expected<br/>2) On the dark side<br/>3) Voters thought it failed to meet the challenge (lighting from behind) - especially note the shadow on the background from her hair and hat (below the bill). <br/> <br/>This is a great picture - it does a great job of capturing your sister''s expression, and, if I may say so, a bit of her personality as well. You''ve done a great job of minimizing distractions (plain background), capturing a nice range of tones, and getting a good depth of field. <br/> <br/>On editing, it looks like you may have introduced a bit of ghosting around the back of her head. If you want a nit - her shirt isn''t straight - against her neck on her right side, as far away from her neck as it could get on her left. Personally, I think that adds a bit to the feel of the picture, and emphasizes the un-posed aspect of the shot. <br/> <br/>As <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=47597" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">balancedfox</a> said, don''t get discouraged. dpchallenge is open to photographers from all over the world. Some folks here are professional photographers - others could easily be. Their equipment ranges all the way from consumer / entry digital cameras to kits costing well over $5000. Here, you aren''t a kid or a "young man" - here, you are a photographer, and your scores are from other photographers who can be hyper-critical. If you are serious about photography, or just want to get pretty good at it for your own enjoyment, this is a great place to learn and grow your photography skills.</td>', 'IMO, your scoring on this got hammered for three main reasons:1) Not what the voters expected2) On the dark side3) Voters thought it failed to meet the challenge (lighting from behind) - especially note the shadow on the background from her hair and hat (below the bill).  This is a great picture - it does a great job of capturing your sister''s expression, and, if I may say so, a bit of her personality as well. You''ve done a great job of minimizing distractions (plain background), capturing a nice range of tones, and getting a good depth of field.  On editing, it looks like you may have introduced a bit of ghosting around the back of her head. If you want a nit - her shirt isn''t straight - against her neck on her right side, as far away from her neck as it could get on her left. Personally, I think that adds a bit to the feel of the picture, and emphasizes the un-posed aspect of the shot.  As  balancedfox said, don''t get discouraged. dpchallenge is open to photographers from all over the world. Some folks here are professional photographers - others could easily be. Their equipment ranges all the way from consumer / entry digital cameras to kits costing well over $5000. Here, you aren''t a kid or a "young man" - here, you are a photographer, and your scores are from other photographers who can be hyper-critical. If you are serious about photography, or just want to get pretty good at it for your own enjoyment, this is a great place to learn and grow your photography skills.', '2012-09-19 16:37:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(6866260, 1033356, 10785, '<td valign="top">Oh say can you see... great light!! Love how you exposed for the highlights with that super high shutter speed and let the rest of the darker tones fall where they may! Also the vignetting in post helped the mood tremendously! Nice work!</td>', 'Oh say can you see... great light!! Love how you exposed for the highlights with that super high shutter speed and let the rest of the darker tones fall where they may! Also the vignetting in post helped the mood tremendously! Nice work!', '2012-09-19 16:49:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(6866271, 1033400, 77196, '<td valign="top">Welcome to dpchallenge! Your shot really got hammered in voting, but I like the car - ''64 Chevy Malibu, right? <br/> <br/>A couple of things going on here that hurt your score - as mentioned in the comments, there is no obvious back lighting (light from behind the left rear of the car). Not meeting the challenge (in voters'' opinions) will absolutely kill your score on dpc. <br/> <br/>Also mentioned in the comments is needing a more interesting perspective. From the angle of the car, it looks like you were standing, raised the camera to your face, and took a picture. Since digital film is cheap, experiment a little - take a shot from ground level, from squatting down, include just the front and front quarter panel in your shot, get something to stand on, and take a picture looking down at the car. <br/>If you have a choice in location, change where the car is located - for example, in a field, in a park, by the beach, along side a country road, just about anywhere but in a neighborhood with other cars competing with yours for attention - the sidewalk cutting diagonally across the front of the picture, the brush in the lower left corner, the house and phone pole in the background - all distract from your picture. <br/> <br/>And, Joe, just a hint here. Many of the voters in these challenges are ladies who are not really interested in cars. To them, this car is just another red car, not something special due to age, year, make, model, condition, etc. <br/>I still submit car pictures, because I like them. But I don''t expect anything close to a good score from them. For example, my entry for Old Cars challenge scored under 5.8.<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=801053" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1053/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_801053.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1053/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_801053.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1053/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_801053.jpg"/></a> <br/> <br/>Again, welcome to dpchallenge - I''m looking forward to seeing more of your work. <br/></td>', 'Welcome to dpchallenge! Your shot really got hammered in voting, but I like the car - ''64 Chevy Malibu, right?  A couple of things going on here that hurt your score - as mentioned in the comments, there is no obvious back lighting (light from behind the left rear of the car). Not meeting the challenge (in voters'' opinions) will absolutely kill your score on dpc.  Also mentioned in the comments is needing a more interesting perspective. From the angle of the car, it looks like you were standing, raised the camera to your face, and took a picture. Since digital film is cheap, experiment a little - take a shot from ground level, from squatting down, include just the front and front quarter panel in your shot, get something to stand on, and take a picture looking down at the car. If you have a choice in location, change where the car is located - for example, in a field, in a park, by the beach, along side a country road, just about anywhere but in a neighborhood with other cars competing with yours for attention - the sidewalk cutting diagonally across the front of the picture, the brush in the lower left corner, the house and phone pole in the background - all distract from your picture.  And, Joe, just a hint here. Many of the voters in these challenges are ladies who are not really interested in cars. To them, this car is just another red car, not something special due to age, year, make, model, condition, etc. I still submit car pictures, because I like them. But I don''t expect anything close to a good score from them. For example, my entry for Old Cars challenge scored under 5.8.  Again, welcome to dpchallenge - I''m looking forward to seeing more of your work. ', '2012-09-19 17:09:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(6866303, 1033497, 103142, '<td valign="top">Foil!?!???! Now you tell me!!! I''ve been trying to roll paper and tape every time I needed a snout. Argh!! Foil makes sooooo much sense!<br/> <br/>Huge congrats on the ribbon, but I''m more excited about foil. :)<br/> <br/>Seriously, though, wonderful shot. :)</td>', 'Foil!?!???! Now you tell me!!! I''ve been trying to roll paper and tape every time I needed a snout. Argh!! Foil makes sooooo much sense! Huge congrats on the ribbon, but I''m more excited about foil. :) Seriously, though, wonderful shot. :)', '2012-09-19 19:03:35.000000', NULL, 0),
